{"role":"assistant","content":"Now writing up the causes in ANSWER.md:","tool_calls":[{"type":"function","id":"tool_thB5vhsmajp72QxTjSFxIWeV","function":{"name":"Write","arguments":"{\"content\":\"# Why the nightly job never reported anything\\n\\nThe job fired on schedule all six nights (loaded 2026-10-12, `launchctl print`\\non 2026-10-18 shows `runs = 6`). Every run failed the same way, and two\\nseparate bugs — one in the script, one in how `claude` authenticates under\\nlaunchd — combined so that no failure note ever reached you.\\n\\n## Cause 1 — the script died before it could ever notify (why you heard nothing)\\n\\n`bin/nightly-review.zsh:19` does:\\n\\n```zsh\\nstatus=$?\\n```\\n\\n`status` is a **read-only special variable in zsh** (its alias for `$?`). The\\nassignment fails with `read-only variable: status` — and in a non-interactive\\nzsh that error **aborts the script on the spot**. Verified by reproducing it:\\nthe script exits with code 1 immediately, never reaching the `notify.sh` call\\non the next line.\\n\\nThis matches the evidence exactly: `Library/Logs/nightly-review.err` contains,\\nsix times, the pair\\n\\n```\\nNot logged in · Please run /login\\n/Users/vk/bin/nightly-review.zsh:19: read-only variable: status\\n```\\n\\nand `launchctl print` shows `last exit code = 1` — the exit status of the\\nkilled script, not of `claude`. So even though the script was *designed* to\\nping you on failure, the failure-handling code was unreachable.\\n\\n**Fix:** renamed the variable to `rc` in `bin/nightly-review.zsh`. (Also added\\na `notify()` wrapper: if `notify.sh` itself fails, the unsent message is\\nechoed to stderr, which launchd captures into `nightly-review.err`.)\\n\\n## Cause 2 — `claude` can't authenticate when launched by launchd (why every run failed)\\n\\nThe first line of each error-log pair, `Not logged in · Please run /login`, is\\nprinted by `claude` itself. On macOS, Claude Code stores its OAuth credential\\nin the login **Keychain**, and a process whose process tree starts at launchd\\ncannot use it — `claude -p` reports \\\"not logged in\\\" from a LaunchAgent even\\nwhen the very same credentials are present, valid, and readable via\\n`security find-generic-password` from that same context. This is a known,\\nlong-standing issue: [anthropics/claude-code#77213](https://github.com/anthropics/claude-code/issues/77213),\\nalso described in [Claude Code works in your terminal but fails in cron](https://openclawdc.com/blog/claude-code-works-in-terminal-fails-in-cron/).\\nRe-running `/login` interactively does **not** fix it — an interactive login is\\nnot a durable credential for a background job.\\n\\n**Fix (partial, from this snapshot):** the script now sources\\n`~/.config/nightly-review.env` if present, with all variables exported to the\\njob. On the real machine, generate a long-lived token once and store it there:\\n\\n```sh\\nclaude setup-token                      # prints a one-year OAuth token\\nmkdir -p ~/.config\\necho 'CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN=<token>' > ~/.config/nightly-review.env\\nchmod 600 ~/.config/nightly-review.env\\n```\\n\\n(`setup-token` is Anthropic's documented mechanism for CI/headless use. The\\ntoken can only make model requests — fine for a commit review. It is kept out\\nof the plist on purpose: plist contents are world-readable and show up in\\n`launchctl print` output.) This step requires your claude.ai account, so it\\ncan't be done from this snapshot.\\n\\n## Cause 3 — `notify.sh` was built to fail silently (would have muted the failure note even after Cause 1 was fixed)\\n\\nTwo latent defects in `bin/notify.sh`:\\n\\n- `set -e` + `TG_BOT_TOKEN:=$(security find-generic-password -s tg-bot -w)`:\\n  if the keychain read fails (locked keychain at 02:30, ACL prompt not\\n  answerable from a background job), the script exits without a word.\\n- `curl` without `--fail` exits 0 even when Telegram replies HTTP 401/400, so\\n  a rejected message looked like success.\\n\\n**Fix:** explicit check that reports `cannot read tg-bot token from keychain`\\nto stderr (→ `nightly-review.err`) and `curl --fail` so HTTP-level failures\\nare non-zero and visible.\\n\\n## What was NOT broken\\n\\nThe plist is valid (`plutil` OK), the schedule fired (`runs = 6`), the custom\\nPATH reached `claude`, and stdout/stderr were captured — the evidence above\\ncomes from those very logs.\\n\\n## Verification done here\\n\\nEnd-to-end runs of old and new scripts with stubbed `claude`/`security`/`curl`\\n(no network, no external calls): the old script reproduces the silent death;\\nthe new script delivers `nightly review FAILED (exit 1), see …` on claude\\nfailure, delivers the summary on success, sources the env file into claude's\\nenvironment, and leaves a clear trace in stderr when the keychain or Telegram\\nis unreachable.\\n\\n## Remaining steps on the real machine\\n\\n1. `claude setup-token`, then write `~/.config/nightly-review.env` as above.\\n2. Scripts are read at run time — no reload needed for them. To test\\n   immediately instead of waiting for 02:30:\\n   `launchctl kickstart -p gui/$UID/com.vk.nightly-review`\\n3. Expect either a review summary or a `FAILED` note on Telegram from then on;\\n   anything still wrong will also be visible in\\n   `~/Library/Logs/nightly-review.err`.\\n\",\"path\":\"ANSWER.md\"}"}}]}
